<section class="story-section" id="problem">
  <p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p>
  <h2>A new task.<br />Another training run?</h2>
  <p>Pretraining gave models a head start. A new task usually still needed labelled examples and <strong>fine-tuning</strong>, updating the model's learned numbers for that task.</p>
  <p>GPT-2 had explored doing tasks through text prompts. Could a larger model go further with a few examples of what you wanted?</p>
</section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea">
  <p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p>
  <h2>The examples are<br />part of the question.</h2>
  <p>In 2020, Tom Brown and colleagues introduced GPT-3 with 175 billion <strong>parameters</strong>, the adjustable numbers learned during training. Like GPT-2, it predicted the next <strong>token</strong>, a piece of text, from earlier text.</p>
  <p>To ask for a translation, put a few word pairs in the <strong>prompt</strong>, the text given to the model. Then leave the next pair unfinished. This is <strong>in-context learning</strong>: using examples inside the prompt to guide the answer, without updating the model’s parameters.</p>
  <p><strong>Zero-shot</strong> means no demonstrations; <strong>one-shot</strong> means one; <strong>few-shot</strong> means several. Larger models often gained more from examples across the paper's tasks. Examples did not help every task, and prompt wording mattered.</p>
