<section class="story-section" id="mattered">
  <p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p>
  <h2>Show what you mean.</h2>
  <p>GPT-3 showed how examples could specify translation, question answering, and other tasks for one model, without a separate fine-tuning run for each.</p>
  <p>Examples can show an answer's format without supplying its facts. Another approach explored in 2020 was to retrieve passages for a model to read. Let's try that next.</p>
</section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper">
  <summary>Go deeper <span>Frozen weights, scale & limits</span></summary>
  <div class="disclosure-body">
    <h3>Conditioning is different from training</h3>
    <p>For demonstrations D, a question q, and answer tokens y, the model assigns <code>Pθ(y | D, q) = ∏t Pθ(yt | D, q, y&lt;t)</code>. θ denotes the learned parameters; y&lt;t means the answer tokens already generated. Changing D changes the computation and next-token probabilities. It does not change θ. Fine-tuning, by contrast, updates θ with a training objective.</p>
    <p>The term “learning” here describes adaptation within a context, not a guarantee of acquiring a new skill. The experiments do not pin down whether each success comes from learning a new task, recognising something seen during pretraining, or another form of conditional behaviour.</p>
    <h3>What was scaled?</h3>
    <p>The study trained eight decoder-only Transformer sizes, from 125 million to 175 billion parameters. A decoder-only model predicts text from earlier tokens. GPT-3 follows GPT-2’s architecture, but alternates dense attention with locally banded sparse attention: some layers look across earlier positions, while others use restricted local patterns.</p>
    <p>All eight models trained on 300 billion sampled tokens. The mixture combined filtered Common Crawl, WebText2, two book corpora, and English Wikipedia. The context window—the text a model can consider at once—held 2,048 tokens. Demonstrations had to fit inside it; the paper typically used 10–100, depending on the task.</p>
    <h3>One result, with its task attached</h3>
    <p>Table 3.2 reports TriviaQA exact-match scores of 64.3% zero-shot, 68.0% one-shot, and 71.2% few-shot for GPT-3. Exact match checks whether the answer matches an accepted reference after normalisation. The few-shot score used the Wikipedia-split test server. This was closed-book question answering: no external passages were retrieved for the answer. These are task-specific results, not a general accuracy score.</p>
    <h3>What the examples could not fix</h3>
    <p>Results varied with the task and prompt. Table 3.1’s one-shot LAMBADA result was worse than its zero-shot result, even though few-shot improved it. The model still struggled with some comparisons and reading-comprehension tasks, and could repeat itself, contradict itself, or lose coherence in long text.</p>
    <p>Some benchmark material overlapped the training data; a filtering bug prevented complete removal. The authors analysed cleaner subsets, but this cannot prove the absence of memorisation. The paper also documented stereotypes in generated text. A useful few-shot answer does not establish truthfulness, fairness, or dependable reasoning.</p>
    <h3>Inside the toy</h3>
    <p>Pair puzzles use four functions: keep a pair in order, swap it, repeat its first word, or repeat its last word. Three-word puzzles use the six possible orders of three words. Only functions matching every visible example survive, and the filter answers once all survivors agree on the new input. Repeated-word examples cannot tell some functions apart; mixed words can. Answering right before the filter scores one point per example you did not need, plus one; matching it scores one. This illustrates how examples specify a task, not GPT-3's internal mechanism.</p>
  </div>
</details>
